WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.316
Safe Rust prevents ordinary double free,

2
00:00:02.396 --> 00:00:05.199
but unsafe code can reintroduce manual
ownership assumptions.

3
00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:08.807
Raw pointers do not automatically record
who owns an allocation, who may free it,

4
00:00:08.887 --> 00:00:11.274
or whether the resource has already been
transferred.

5
00:00:11.354 --> 00:00:13.378
Those facts live in the surrounding
contract.

6
00:00:13.458 --> 00:00:17.091
Manual reconstruction of owned values from
raw pointers is especially sensitive.

7
00:00:17.171 --> 00:00:20.751
The review question is whether ownership
is being reconstructed exactly once and

8
00:00:20.831 --> 00:00:22.803
only by the code that truly owns the
resource.

9
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:25.115
If two wrappers are built around the same
raw owner,

10
00:00:25.195 --> 00:00:27.219
cleanup may run twice through separate
paths.

11
00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:29.634
Custom allocation behavior needs clear
boundaries.

12
00:00:29.714 --> 00:00:31.323
Which allocator created the memory?

13
00:00:31.403 --> 00:00:33.375
Which component is allowed to deallocate
it?

14
00:00:33.455 --> 00:00:36.880
Has ownership been transferred to Rust, to
external code, or to a wrapper type?

15
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.814
The answer should be visible in the API
and safety documentation.

16
00:00:39.894 --> 00:00:43.059
Advanced tools such as `ManuallyDrop`,
intentional drop suppression,

17
00:00:43.139 --> 00:00:45.890
or manual cleanup sequencing require
strong review evidence.

18
00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:48.201
They can be legitimate in low-level
abstractions,

19
00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:50.876
but they should not be used to paper over
unclear ownership.

20
00:00:50.956 --> 00:00:54.277
The safety contract should explain why
ordinary `Drop` is not enough and how

21
00:00:54.357 --> 00:00:55.810
duplicate cleanup is prevented.

22
00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.626
A safe wrapper around unsafe internals
must enforce cleanup invariants for ordinary

23
00:00:59.706 --> 00:01:00.121
callers.

24
00:01:00.201 --> 00:01:03.159
Callers should not be able to create two
owners for one raw resource,

25
00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:04.485
free through the wrong path,

26
00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:07.108
or keep using an object after ownership
has been consumed.

27
00:01:07.188 --> 00:01:08.900
The wrapper is the defensive boundary.
